// src/v4/extdeps/languages/ptx.dag
//
// Scope: CUDA model — direction-agnostic. Modeled at the PTX ISA layer
//        (forced by DECISIONS.md L-3 — PTX is the SIMT *IR*; CUDA-C++ is
//        a Shape A source already covered by general modeling, so it
//        would not be a down-the-stack probe at all): the SIMT execution
//        model (grid / block / thread), the memory hierarchy
//        (register / shared / global / etc.), kernels and barriers.
// Anchor: NVIDIA "Parallel Thread Execution ISA" specification — pin a
//         version (e.g. PTX ISA 8.x) as the versioned fidelity authority
//         (L-2); the "CUDA C++ Programming Guide" documents the SIMT
//         execution model the spec encodes.
//
// IN-B FINDING (the T-4.14 probe's deliverable, operator-ratified
//   STOP-or-find discipline per §D): SIMT data-parallelism IS modelable
//   as the existing 5 L1 behaviors — NO 6th `Parallel` / `Kernel`
//   behavior is required. The full mapping lives in the modeling notes
//   section below; the headline:
//     kernel        → Transform (a function over (thread_coord, params))
//     thread grid   → Loop (bounded recursion over the grid's bounded
//                     thread-index cardinality; THESIS:18 "parallelism
//                     is the default" — independence is read from the
//                     dependency graph, scheduling is mechanical)
//     barrier       → Bind (sequencing synchronization point)
//     state space   → effect-typed parameter (a structural Conj field
//                     on the value, per THESIS:401 IN-B "intrinsic to
//                     the type signature, not an annotation layer")
//     @p predicate  → Branch (predicated execution = guarded Branch on
//                     a `.pred` carrier)
//   The 5 behaviors hold; the probe surfaces no substrate gap (no STOP).
//
// Owns:
//   - PTX coordinate classifiers as closed structural carriers:
//     StateSpace (memory hierarchy), BarrierScope (CTA/GPU/system),
//     ThreadAxis (X/Y/Z), ThreadCoordSource (thread-in-CTA / CTA-in-grid),
//     RegisterWidth (b1/b8/b16/b32/b64/b128), RegisterScalarKind
//     (Bits/Unsigned/Signed/Float/Predicate)
//   - PTX value shapes as Conj records: RegisterScalar, Dim3 (🟡 scaffold
//     — same bridge as std/diagnostic.dag's ByteRange),
//     ThreadHierarchyShape, ThreadCoord
//   - PTX cost-realization carrier shape: PtxCost (per-instruction +
//     occupancy components — the per-target cost projection U1 reads)
//   - the IN-B mapping (modeling notes): SIMT → 5 behaviors, no 6th
//
// Owned ELSEWHERE (do not duplicate here):
//   - the bidirectional LanguageModel substrate (grammar productions,
//     C5-fidelity disposition tags, ingest∘emit roundtrip carrier) — the
//     BUNDLED work of T-4 across the 5 Shape A source languages, where
//     "the SHAPE is the work" (TASKS.md T-4 authoring contract). When
//     that shape lands, this file's classifiers slot into the PTX
//     `LanguageModel` instance; the classifiers themselves do not
//     prejudge that shape.
//   - PTX integer / float scalar grounding (`.u32` inhabits `Semiring`,
//     `.s32` inhabits `OrderedRing`, `.f32` inhabits `ApproximateField`)
//     — flows from std/integer.dag / std/machine.dag / std/float.dag
//     (T-3 wave A3) when those carriers + their algebra instance-values
//     land. The PTX register-type vocabulary (kind × width) declared
//     here is the SURFACE through which that grounding parameterizes.
//     Pre-T-3-A3 this file declares no algebra instance-value, by P2
//     single-authority: re-declaring an inhabitance whose carrier file
//     is unwritten would foreclose its eventual authority.
//   - SIMT-as-Bind composition's effect-typing carrier — that is the
//     subject of extdeps/coordination.dag (T-4.8); the IN-B finding
//     here demonstrates the mapping fits the 5 behaviors, and
//     coordination.dag (when it lands) owns the effect-typed Bind
//     carrier the kernel-as-Transform composes through.
//
// Consumes:
//   - nothing — this file is closed-enum + Conj-record declarations
//     only. `Int` is kernel-ambient (Dim3 component, RegisterWidth bit-
//     count surface). No algebra instance-values are declared at this
//     phase (see "Owned ELSEWHERE" — PTX scalar inhabitance is
//     downstream of T-3 wave A3 + T-4's LanguageModel shape).
//   (The scaffold header's prior `std/node.dag, std/algebra.dag,
//   std/primitive.dag` Consumes line predated PR #3152's
//   std/primitive.dag decomposition AND the operator-ratified §0
//   scope-narrowing of this probe to "closed classifiers + IN-B
//   finding"; the revised Consumes line above reflects what the file
//   actually imports, not the scaffold's anticipatory list.)
//
// Status: T-4.14 modeled 2026-05-16 (gentle-deer-446); probe finding:
// NO 6th behavior surfaced. The I/O contract in this header is
// immutable for downstream workers. Splitting this file requires
// explicit operator ratification (substrate extension = stop signal).


module v4.extdeps.languages.ptx


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Modeling notes (read before validating against the anchor)
//
// THE IN-B FALSIFICATION PROBE (T-4.14, B2-OMNI + IN-B, DECISIONS.md
// L-3). The brief's §D framing is binding: "the STOP discipline is the
// deliverable — if the substrate (5 behaviors / 6 connectives / fail-
// closed) genuinely can't model the target, that is a substrate
// finding to surface, not force." For T-4.14: "STOP if tempted toward
// a 6th behavior." This section records the structural fit and
// names the operator-tier triggers that would have flipped the
// finding to STOP — neither fired.
//
// PROBE OUTCOME — the 5 behaviors hold; the SIMT model fits without a
// 6th. The mapping, member-by-member:
//
//   - KERNEL ↦ Transform.
//     A PTX `.entry` is a function `(thread_coord, kernel_params) ->
//     side-effects-on-state-space`. That is exactly the Transform
//     behavior (function application — INVARIANTS:72 typed-total-λ
//     fragment, node.dag:209 "Transform=application"). The kernel's
//     body is a sub-DAG of the 5 behaviors, identical to a CPU
//     function's body — no kernel-specific behavior surfaces.
//     "Run once per thread" is the SCHEDULING of the surrounding Loop
//     (see next), not a property of the kernel function itself; the
//     Transform IS the kernel callable.
//
//   - THREAD GRID ↦ Loop.
//     The execution-of-a-kernel-across-its-grid is bounded recursion
//     over the grid's bounded thread-index cardinality (gridDim ×
//     blockDim, both Cardinality-bounded). This is exactly the A2
//     `Loop` discipline (node.dag:42-68: bounded recursion, total-by-
//     construction). Per THESIS:18 / node.dag:209 "Parallelism is the
//     default because independence is visible in the structure" —
//     iterations of the kernel-Loop over distinct thread-coords are
//     INDEPENDENT unless they communicate (through `.shared` /
//     `.global` reads/writes or barriers, both visible structural
//     edges). The SIMT schedule (lock-step warp execution) is the
//     mechanical realization of the dependency graph; it is NOT a
//     separate behavior. Tempting alternative: a `Parallel` /
//     `Kernel` behavior carrying "this Loop is data-parallel". That
//     temptation would have been the STOP trigger — but it duplicates
//     what the dependency graph already says (THESIS:18 again:
//     "sequential execution is what needs justification"). Adding
//     such a behavior would be a feedback_no_annotations violation
//     (annotating a property structurally already visible). REJECTED;
//     no STOP needed.
//
//   - BARRIER ↦ Bind.
//     `bar.sync` / `membar` join concurrent threads at a
//     synchronization point. That is exactly the Bind behavior
//     (node.dag:209 "Bind=let" — sequencing). A barrier's SCOPE (CTA
//     / GPU / system) is a Conj-record field on the Bind (the
//     `BarrierScope` classifier below), not a new behavior; same
//     pattern as `bar.sync 0` vs `membar.gl` differing only in scope.
//     Tempting alternative: a `Synchronize` behavior. REJECTED — Bind
//     is the existing sequencing primitive; "synchronize across
//     concurrent threads" is a property of the surrounding parallel
//     Loop reading the Bind, not of the Bind itself.
//
//   - STATE SPACE ↦ effect-typed parameter (structural Conj field).
//     A PTX value's `.reg` / `.shared` / `.global` / etc. tag is the
//     `state_space: StateSpace` field on its `RegisterScalar` Conj
//     record (`StateSpace` is the closed classifier declared below;
//     `RegisterScalar` is the Conj record that attaches it to the
//     typed value). Memory ops (`ld.global`, `st.shared`) are
//     Transforms whose input/output StateSpace tags constrain valid
//     composition. This IS the THESIS:401 IN-B claim made concrete:
//     effects are "intrinsic to the type signature, NOT an
//     annotation layer." Tempting alternative: a `StateSpaceTransfer`
//     behavior wrapping every ld/st. REJECTED — it would parallel-
//     represent what the Transform's signature already says; the
//     effect-typed parameter is the unannotated primitive.
//
//   - @p PREDICATE ↦ Branch.
//     PTX predicated execution (`@p mov.b32 r1, r2;`) is a guarded
//     Branch on the `.pred` carrier: `if p then op else skip`. The
//     `Branch` behavior already covers this (node.dag:209
//     "Branch=sums"). Predicate negation (`@!p`) is the
//     `BooleanAlgebra<.pred>.complement` instance-value — to land
//     downstream when std/logic.dag's inhabitance form lands;
//     declaring it here would parallel-represent it pre-substrate
//     (P2). For T-4.14 the structural fit is the deliverable; the
//     instance-value follows.
//
// CONCLUSION. The probe's IN-B half closes with no STOP. The B2-OMNI
// half (PTX grammar as declarative bidirectional data) hinges on
// T-4's LanguageModel shape — when that bundled shape lands, this
// file's coordinate classifiers slot into the PTX `LanguageModel`
// instance without revision (their I/O contract is the immutable
// header above).
//
// SCOPE NEGATIVE (operator-tier, do not work around): warp-level
// intrinsics (`%warpid`, `%laneid`, `%smid`), texture / surface
// references, async copies (`cp.async`), tensor cores (`wmma`,
// `mma.sync`), and the `bf16` / vector-pack types (`.f16x2`,
// `.f32x2`) are deliberately not modeled at T-4.14. They sit either
// (a) inside the bundled LanguageModel shape (grammar productions —
// T-4's work), (b) inside the cost-realization detail per release
// pin (PtxCost note below), or (c) inside the SIMT-effect-typed Bind
// substrate that coordination.dag (T-4.8) owns. The classifiers
// declared here are the irreducible SIMT coordinate set; the rest
// composes over them when their substrate lands.
//
// PRACTICE-4 LEDGER DISCIPLINE. Every closed-Disj carrier below
// carries the full 🟢/🟡/🔴 + all five patterns (Fact-placement /
// Variant-is-data / Algebraic-form / Dimensional / Parameterized-
// family-Practice-7) per modeling-discipline.md §4 (operator-ratified
// binding 2026-05-15, gate (b) — #3163 made the 4-pattern miss the
// proof case; do not omit Practice-7). Conj records carry no ledger
// (only coproducts dissolve); scaffold-residual Conj fields (Dim3
// raw Int offsets) carry a 🟡 bridge note mirroring std/diagnostic.dag's
// `Extent.ByteRange` precedent.


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// StateSpace — the closed PTX memory hierarchy classifier. Exactly
// eight; the surface vocabulary of the PTX state-space declaration
// directive (e.g. `.global .f32 v;`).
//
//   - Reg       — `.reg`     virtual register (per-thread; the
//                            register-allocated value's home)
//   - SReg      — `.sreg`    special register (read-only thread-coord
//                            and warp/lane intrinsics; per-thread)
//   - Const     — `.const`   constant memory (read-only; per-grid)
//   - Global    — `.global`  global memory (read-write; per-grid)
//   - Local     — `.local`   local memory (per-thread spill region)
//   - Param     — `.param`   kernel parameter memory (read-only;
//                            kernel-launch staging)
//   - Shared    — `.shared`  shared memory (read-write; per-CTA)
//   - Tex       — `.tex`     texture memory (read-only; per-grid)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A ninth state space is an edit to THIS
// declaration — a visible diff routed to operator ratification. The
// PTX spec adds state spaces across releases (e.g. recent additions
// for cooperative-group memory); the pin discipline (L-2 versioned
// fidelity authority) means each release-version model EDITS this
// declaration explicitly, never silently extends it.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every memory-op Transform branches on
//      the operand's StateSpace (`ld.global` vs `ld.shared` are
//      distinct ops); shared classifier, not a per-consumer scattered
//      fact.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: the eight are nullary tags drawn
//      from a closed PTX-defined set. Promoting to a `{ name:
//      Symbol }` record reintroduces the readable-string-as-tag
//      heuristic surface K-1 forecloses (a `match name { "global" =>
//      … }` smell). The enum IS the faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: PTX state spaces are
//      the PTX spec's primitive memory-region taxonomy; they do not
//      trace to a richer std/ algebraic structure (no Lattice / no
//      Field on the eight tags — read-only vs read-write is a
//      separate per-state-space property, not a partial order over
//      them).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a PTX value lives in EXACTLY ONE state
//      space; the directive `.global .shared` is a syntax error in
//      PTX itself. Mutually-exclusive alternatives, not simultaneous
//      coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the eight have structurally
//      distinct semantic roles (per-thread vs per-CTA vs per-grid;
//      readable vs read-only; addressable vs intrinsic-only) — NOT
//      one generic `F<X>` / projection over a source set (Practice
//      7). They are the PTX spec's irreducible memory-region
//      partition, not a mechanical copy of any enumerable set.
// Terminal: the closed eight-way memory-hierarchy classifier; a 9th
// is the C1 stop-signal (a visible ratified edit to this declaration,
// STRUCTURE.md commitment #1).
type StateSpace
  = Reg
  | SReg
  | Const
  | Global
  | Local
  | Param
  | Shared
  | Tex


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// BarrierScope — the closed PTX synchronization scope classifier. The
// scope at which a Bind-as-barrier synchronizes (`bar.sync` / `membar`
// / `fence` directives carry exactly one of four scopes per the
// pinned PTX ISA 8.x §8.5 memory-consistency model).
//
//   - Cta      — `.cta`      within-CTA (within-block) synchronization
//                            (`bar.sync 0;` is `.cta`-scoped by default;
//                            `membar.cta` / `fence.acq_rel.cta`)
//   - Cluster  — `.cluster`  thread-block-cluster scope (PTX 7.8+,
//                            Hopper / CUDA 9.0 thread block clusters;
//                            `membar.cluster` / `fence.*.cluster` — a
//                            cluster groups multiple CTAs that share
//                            distributed shared memory)
//   - Gpu      — `.gpu` (alias `.gl`)
//                            device-wide (within-GPU) memory ordering
//                            (`membar.gl` / `fence.*.gpu`)
//   - System   — `.sys`      system-wide (across host/device) ordering
//                            (`membar.sys` / `fence.*.sys`)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A fifth scope is an edit to THIS
// declaration routed to operator ratification under the L-2 pin
// discipline (the pinned PTX ISA version is the fidelity authority
// — a future ISA release introducing a fifth scope is a visible diff
// to this enum, never a silent extension).
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every Bind-as-barrier reads the scope
//      uniformly (the synchronization radius is the same datum every
//      consumer needs); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: nullary tags from a closed PTX-defined
//      set. A `{ radius: Int }` proxy (1=cta, 2=cluster, 3=gpu,
//      4=sys) replaces a closed 4-valued exhaustive sum with an
//      unbounded Int (radius=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4
//      (closed sets are enums, not numeric proxies). Same shape as
//      algebra.dag's `Ordering` ledger.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the four scopes are the
//      PTX memory-fence primitive radii; they do not trace to a
//      richer std/ algebraic structure (a `BoundedLattice<Scope>`
//      with Cta ⊑ Cluster ⊑ Gpu ⊑ System would be a parallel
//      representation of "stronger fence subsumes weaker" — a derived
//      property of the tags, not a richer source).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single barrier directive carries
//      EXACTLY ONE scope; `membar.cta.gl` is not a PTX construct.
//      Mutually-exclusive alternatives, not coordinates.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the four are distinct
//      synchronization-radius primitives — NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a source set (Practice 7). The four radii are
//      the PTX spec's irreducible scope partition for the pinned ISA
//      version.
// Terminal: the closed four-way synchronization scope for the pinned
// PTX ISA 8.x §8.5 spec.
type BarrierScope
  = Cta
  | Cluster
  | Gpu
  | System


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadAxis — the closed PTX coordinate axis classifier. PTX exposes
// thread / block indices as 3-vectors (`%tid.x/.y/.z`, `%ctaid.x/.y/.z`);
// ThreadAxis is the axis SELECTOR for one of those components.
//
//   - X — the x-axis component (the SIMT-fast axis: lanes within a
//         warp vary along x first; the layout convention threadIdx.x
//         carries)
//   - Y — the y-axis component
//   - Z — the z-axis component
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). PTX has fixed 3-D thread/block addressing
// across all current versions; a fourth axis would be a substantive
// ISA change routed to operator ratification.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every ThreadCoord consumer that reads
//      a specific axis (e.g. emission of `%tid.x`) branches on the
//      axis tag; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ component: Int }` proxy (0/1/2)
//      replaces a closed 3-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int (component=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4 the same
//      way `Ordering` does (algebra.dag:332). The enum IS the
//      faithful form.
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: X/Y/Z are the basis
//      labels of a 3-D coordinate vector; they do not trace to a
//      richer std/ structure (no algebra over the labels themselves;
//      the algebra is on the bounded-Int dim values, separately).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a `.x` / `.y` / `.z` axis selector picks
//      EXACTLY ONE axis; mutually-exclusive, not simultaneous
//      coordinates. (`%tid` as a whole vector is the Dim3 record,
//      separate from the per-axis selector.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: X/Y/Z are positional labels of
//      a fixed 3-D vector — NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection over
//      a source set (Practice 7). They are the closed three-axis
//      basis, not a copy of any enumerable set.
// Terminal: the closed three-way axis selector of PTX's 3-D
// coordinate addressing.
type ThreadAxis
  = X
  | Y
  | Z


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadCoordSource — the closed PTX thread-coordinate hierarchy
// classifier. PTX exposes two structural levels of thread coordinate:
// position within the CTA, and CTA position within the grid. Warp /
// lane / SM intrinsics (`%warpid`, `%laneid`, `%smid`) are scope-
// negative for T-4.14 (see header SCOPE NEGATIVE) — they are
// realizations of the SIMT schedule, not a coordinate axis the model
// admits as substrate.
//
//   - ThreadInCta — `%tid.{x,y,z}` — thread position within the CTA
//                                    (block)
//   - CtaInGrid   — `%ctaid.{x,y,z}` — CTA position within the grid
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A third hierarchy level (e.g. cluster — a
// CUDA 9.0+ surface that bundles CTAs) is an edit to THIS declaration
// routed to operator ratification under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every ThreadCoord consumer reads the
//      source uniformly to know which intrinsic register family it
//      emits; shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ level: Int }` (0=thread,
//      1=cta) replaces a closed exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int — violates P2 + M4 (algebra.dag:332 `Ordering`
//      precedent).
//   3. Algebraic form — N/A by construction: the two hierarchy
//      levels are the PTX coordinate-space partition; they do not
//      trace to a richer std/ structure (one could attempt a
//      `PartialOrder<ThreadCoordSource>` with ThreadInCta ⊑
//      CtaInGrid, but that would be a parallel representation of
//      what is already a structural containment — feedback_no_engine
//      parallel-rep smell).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single coordinate read is EITHER
//      thread-in-cta OR cta-in-grid; mutually-exclusive
//      alternatives, not simultaneous coordinates. (A kernel that
//      uses both addresses each through a separate read; it does
//      not co-locate the two.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the two are distinct
//      coordinate-space partitions — NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a source set (Practice 7).
// Terminal: the closed two-way thread-coordinate hierarchy at the
// SIMT-substrate layer the probe models.
type ThreadCoordSource
  = ThreadInCta
  | CtaInGrid


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RegisterWidth — the closed PTX bit-width classifier for typed
// registers. Exactly six widths admitted by the PTX scalar type
// system (`.b{n}` / `.u{n}` / `.s{n}` / `.f{n}`):
//
//   - Bits1   — `.pred`        the 1-bit predicate register width
//                              (only the Predicate kind admits this)
//   - Bits8   — `.{b,u,s}8`
//   - Bits16  — `.{b,u,s,f}16` (`.f16` is IEEE-754 half-precision)
//   - Bits32  — `.{b,u,s,f}32`
//   - Bits64  — `.{b,u,s,f}64`
//   - Bits128 — `.b128`        (untyped only at this width)
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A 256-bit / 512-bit / bf16-distinct
// addition is an edit to THIS declaration (and the kind-width
// admissibility table) under the L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every RegisterScalar consumer that
//      emits or sizes a register reads the width tag; shared
//      classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ bits: Int }` proxy (1/8/16/…)
//      replaces a closed 6-valued exhaustive sum with an unbounded
//      Int (bits=42 representable) — violates P2 + M4. The widths
//      are NOT free Int values; they are the PTX-admitted subset,
//      so a free-Int carrier would ratify illegal widths (the
//      INVARIANTS P2 illegal-states-unrepresentable shape).
//   3. Algebraic form — partially N/A: the bit-counts form an
//      ordered set (1 < 8 < 16 < …), but the ORDER is derived FROM
//      the closed enum, not a richer source — pointing to a
//      `Lattice<RegisterWidth>` would parallel-represent the
//      enumeration the order is defined over (algebra.dag U1 engine-
//      debt smell).
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single typed register has EXACTLY ONE
//      width; mutually-exclusive alternatives. The kind × width
//      Cartesian product lives on `RegisterScalar` (Conj), not on
//      this enum.
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the six widths are the PTX-
//      admitted bit-counts; they are NOT one generic `F<X>` /
//      projection over a separately-declared source set (Practice
//      7). The bit-count itself IS the discriminant; there is no
//      richer X to project from.
// Terminal: the closed six-way PTX register-width classifier.
type RegisterWidth
  = Bits1
  | Bits8
  | Bits16
  | Bits32
  | Bits64
  | Bits128


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RegisterScalarKind — the closed PTX scalar-type kind classifier.
// PTX scalar types decompose as KIND × WIDTH (the kind names the
// interpretation; the width sizes it):
//
//   - Bits      — `.b{w}`   untyped bit-vector (width-only, no
//                           arithmetic semantics)
//   - Unsigned  — `.u{w}`   unsigned modular integer (Semiring)
//   - Signed    — `.s{w}`   signed two's-complement integer
//                           (OrderedRing)
//   - Float     — `.f{w}`   IEEE-754 floating-point (ApproximateField;
//                           w ∈ {16, 32, 64})
//   - Predicate — `.pred`   1-bit boolean (BooleanAlgebra; width is
//                           always Bits1, so the kind alone
//                           determines width)
//
// The KIND × WIDTH admissibility (e.g. `.f8` is invalid; `.b128` is
// the only Bits-kind width above 64; `.pred` admits only Bits1) is a
// `RegisterScalar`-record-level structural invariant, evaluated
// fail-closed by a `register_scalar_well_formed` check whose home is
// the LanguageModel grammar substrate (T-4). The kind enum here
// closes the K dimension; widths close the W dimension; the cross-
// product admissibility is the joint check, not a separate carrier.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A sixth kind (e.g. brain-float `bf` as a
// kind distinct from Float) is an edit to THIS declaration under the
// L-2 pin discipline.
//
// Coproduct dissolution (Practice 4 / modeling-discipline.md §4) —
// 🟢 GREEN (terminal). Ledger — five patterns attempted:
//   1. Fact placement — FAILS: every RegisterScalar consumer reads
//      the kind to dispatch to the right algebra instance (which
//      operations apply); shared classifier, not scattered.
//   2. Variant-is-data — FAILS: a `{ name: Symbol }` proxy reopens
//      the readable-string heuristic surface K-1 forecloses (a
//      `match name { "u32" => … }` smell). The enum IS the faithful
//      form.
//   3. Algebraic form — PARTIAL: each kind grounds in a std/algebra
//      structure (Unsigned→Semiring, Signed→OrderedRing,
//      Float→ApproximateField, Predicate→BooleanAlgebra, Bits→no
//      arithmetic algebra by design). That grounding is an
//      INHABITANCE relation — owned by the inhabiting carrier file
//      (std/integer.dag for Unsigned/Signed at width w,
//      std/float.dag for Float, std/logic.dag for Predicate) per
//      P2. The kind enum itself stays a closed classifier; the
//      "kind → algebra" map IS the future inhabitance walk, not a
//      richer std/ source to collapse this enum INTO.
//   4. Dimensional — FAILS: a single scalar register has EXACTLY
//      ONE kind; mutually-exclusive alternatives. (Kind × Width is
//      a Cartesian product on `RegisterScalar`, which IS the
//      dimensional model — these are the K coordinates.)
//   5. Parameterized family — FAILS: the five are the PTX-spec
//      kind partition; they are NOT one generic `F<X>` / projection
//      over a separately-declared source set (Practice 7). One
//      could attempt `Kind<algebra: AlgebraStructure>` parameterized
//      over an algebra-structure set, but only four of the five
//      ground in algebra (Bits has no arithmetic algebra by design);
//      the partition does not project from a single source set.
// Terminal: the closed five-way PTX scalar-kind classifier.
type RegisterScalarKind
  = Bits
  | Unsigned
  | Signed
  | Float
  | Predicate


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// RegisterScalar — a typed PTX scalar register carrier: kind × width
// × state-space residence. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger — only
// coproducts dissolve).
//
//   - kind          which scalar-type kind (`.b` / `.u` / `.s` / `.f`
//                   / `.pred`)
//   - width         which bit-width (`.b8` / `.b16` / … / `.b128`)
//                   — the kind × width admissibility (e.g. `.pred`
//                   admits only Bits1; `.f8` is invalid) is the
//                   T-4-owned `register_scalar_well_formed` joint
//                   check (see RegisterScalarKind note above)
//   - state_space   where the register-bound value lives in the
//                   memory hierarchy (the effect-typed parameter per
//                   the IN-B mapping — see header IN-B FINDING)
//
// The state_space FIELD on this record is the structural surface of
// the IN-B claim "state space is an effect-typed parameter, intrinsic
// to the type signature, NOT an annotation layer" (THESIS:401,
// scaffold header IN-B note). Memory-op Transforms read this field
// directly from operand types; there is no separate state-space-
// annotation layer.
type RegisterScalar {
  kind: RegisterScalarKind
  width: RegisterWidth
  state_space: StateSpace
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Dim3 — the PTX 3-D dimension/index carrier: an x/y/z triple of
// kernel-launch dimensions or coordinate indices. Conj record (no
// Practice-4 ledger — only coproducts dissolve).
//
//   - x, y, z       the three-axis components
//
// 🟡 SCAFFOLD (same bridge as std/diagnostic.dag's `Extent.ByteRange`).
// With only kernel-ambient `Int` available at the T-4.14 phase,
// `{ x: Int, y: Int, z: Int }` STRUCTURALLY ADMITS illegal states
// (negative dims; zero dims; dims exceeding the PTX max per axis —
// 65535 for x of gridDim, 1024 for thread count of blockDim, etc.).
// modeling-discipline.md §2 (illegal-states-unrepresentable) forbids
// admitting them; no bounded-Cardinality refinement substrate exists
// yet to exclude them. It is a TRACKED scaffold, declared with the
// three bridge properties (the precedent diagnostic.dag established
// 2026-05-15):
//   (a) scaffold doc — THIS note; the carrier is provisional, not
//       the terminal bounded-axis shape.
//   (b) bounds on use — a producer MUST construct only valid PTX
//       dimensions per the pinned PTX-ISA version's per-axis limits.
//       Until the substrate enforces it the invariant is producer-
//       side; a Dim3 violating it is a producer bug, not a legal
//       value.
//   (c) dissolution trigger — when std/cardinality.dag's refinement
//       substrate lands (T-3, P4 decidability), `x` / `y` / `z`
//       become refined bounded-positive-Int axes (positive and
//       within the PTX-version-pinned per-axis maxima), and this
//       scaffold dissolves.
type Dim3 {
  x: Int
  y: Int
  z: Int
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadHierarchyShape — a PTX kernel launch's structural shape: the
// grid's CTA-count dimensions × the per-CTA thread-count dimensions.
// Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
//   - grid_dim      number of CTAs along x/y/z in the grid
//                   (`gridDim.{x,y,z}`)
//   - block_dim     number of threads along x/y/z within each CTA
//                   (`blockDim.{x,y,z}`)
//
// The product `grid_dim × block_dim` is the BOUNDED CARDINALITY of
// the thread-grid Loop per the IN-B mapping (kernel iteration runs
// over this finite set — the A2 boundedness that lets the Loop be
// total-by-construction).
type ThreadHierarchyShape {
  grid_dim: Dim3
  block_dim: Dim3
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// ThreadCoord — a typed thread-coordinate read: a specific axis
// component of a specific hierarchy level. Conj record (no Practice-4
// ledger).
//
//   - source        which hierarchy level (thread-in-CTA or CTA-in-
//                   grid; warp / lane / SM are scope-negative for
//                   T-4.14)
//   - axis          which 3-D axis component (X / Y / Z)
//
// Concretely: `ThreadCoord { source: ThreadInCta, axis: X }` is the
// `%tid.x` intrinsic; `{ source: CtaInGrid, axis: Z }` is `%ctaid.z`.
type ThreadCoord {
  source: ThreadCoordSource
  axis: ThreadAxis
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// PtxCost — the per-target cost-realization carrier shape for the
// PTX `LanguageModel`. Conj record (no Practice-4 ledger).
//
// This declares the SHAPE the U1 spine reads when computing PTX-side
// realization cost — both components per the scaffold header's
// "per-instruction + occupancy cost on a PTX target" claim:
//
//   - instruction_cost   per-instruction throughput cost (the U1
//                        per-target-op cost projection — the `map`
//                        side of `Homomorphism<…, …, PtxScalar>`
//                        carries this; cost is read FROM the map's
//                        structure, never stored as a parallel field
//                        per algebra.dag's U1 no-engine discipline)
//   - occupancy_cost     per-kernel occupancy cost (registers-per-
//                        thread × threads-per-block, shared-memory-
//                        per-block) — the SIMT-specific cost
//                        contribution beyond per-instruction; how
//                        many warps the SM can co-schedule
//
// Both Int carriers are scaffold (same 🟡 bridge as Dim3): cost is a
// non-negative quantity, but without std/cardinality.dag refinement
// negative-cost is structurally representable. Producer-side
// invariant pending the cardinality substrate landing. PTX-ISA-
// version-specific cost tables (e.g. SASS instruction latencies per
// release) sit in the inhabitance walk that T-4's LanguageModel
// shape parameterizes, not in this carrier itself.
type PtxCost {
  instruction_cost: Int
  occupancy_cost: Int
}
